Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for the CraftHaven project as requested.

## Changes Made:

### 1. Installed @vercel/analytics Package
- Added `@vercel/analytics` version 2.0.1 to the project dependencies
- Used npm install to add the package and update lock files

### 2. Integrated Analytics Component
- Modified `src/App.jsx` to import the Analytics component from '@vercel/analytics/react'
- Added `<Analytics />` component at the root level of the App component, just before the closing div
- Followed the official Vercel Web Analytics React/Vite quickstart guide

### Files Modified:
- `frontend/package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `frontend/package-lock.json` - Updated with new package dependencies
- `frontend/src/App.jsx` - Added Analytics import and component

## Implementation Details:

Following the official Vercel documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), I implemented the React/Vite integration pattern:

1. Imported the Analytics component: `import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/react';`
2. Placed the component at the root level of the App component for proper tracking across the entire application

The Analytics component will automatically track page views and user interactions once the application is deployed to Vercel.

## Verification:

✅ Build successful - The project builds without errors (verified with `npm run build`)
✅ Dependencies installed - package.json and package-lock.json properly updated
✅ Code structure preserved - No existing functionality was affected
✅ Framework-specific implementation - Used the correct React/Vite integration pattern

Note: The linter shows 2 pre-existing warnings about unused variables (`currentSlide` and `setCurrentSlide`) that are unrelated to the Analytics integration.

## Next Steps:

To enable Analytics in production:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics tab)
2. Deploy the application to Vercel
3. Verify Analytics are working by checking for network requests to the Vercel analytics endpoint
